{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791581188,"nonce":"WE73n4o/QlYNJIK1W/Q5yZtUjTc=","message":"Where the post-disposal memory sits (2026-10-09, Node 24.2.0, M-series Mac, release addon at 3f30e5e; 100,000 items, six make/dispose rounds; per item).\n\n**Most of it is V8's young generation, kept committed after a busy round.** v8.getHeapSpaceStatistics after disposal and GC, change from the start of the process, [committed, used]:\n- closure: new space [294, 0], old [-3, 0]\n- effect: new space [629, 0], old [-3, 0], large objects [9, 9]\nNothing live is left on the V8 heap (used is 0 to 11 B). V8 grows the young generation while a round keeps many objects alive across scavenges, and does not shrink it afterwards: not with GC alone, and not after 20 s idle. It is not a fixed cap: with 300,000 effects it stayed about 430 B per item. With --max-semi-space-size=1 it is gone, and a memory-reducing GC (gc({flavor: 'last-resort'})) hands it back: footprint then falls from about 1,850 B to 255-305 B per effect (closures: 470 to 66-73). Effects push it higher than closures because each effect's first run allocates more short-lived objects (a Scope, the evaluation record, closures). This is V8's sizing policy, not something the SDK holds; it is per process, not per effect.\n\n**The native part is retained capacity, reused by the next round.** vmmap dirty+swapped change after disposal: MALLOC_SMALL about 112-139 B and MALLOC_LARGE 104 B per effect (closures: about 23-49). Ours is about 40 B of that: the addon's slot table (32 B a slot), free list and effect-slot map (4 B each), kept at the peak size so the next round reuses them. The rest is Blinc's: its effect slot map, the source signal's subscriber list (100,000 subscribers at the peak), and the due and pending queues. Rounds level off, so none of it grows.\n\n**Changed**: benchmarks/reactive-memory.mjs now reports a settled split (in 7963495): V8 committed heap, the rest, and the footprint after a memory-reducing GC. Same run: effect settled {v8Committed 1310, rest 535, afterReducingGc 296}; closure {296, 135, 51}; signal {296, 188, 222}; computed {1319, 354, 408}.\n\nNot fixing anything here: the young-generation space is V8 policy, and the native capacity is deliberate reuse. Closing, with the live cost per effect at 856-954 B (was about 1,700 B when this was filed).","files":null}]}